# Exam track — Describing a Picture: Living Room — Sub-lesson 1: General Description
#
# The photo: a family of four having coffee in a cosy living room. Three sit on
# an olive couch (a teenage girl left, a woman in a knitted jumper, a man in a
# dark-green jumper), a fourth sits in a boucle armchair on the right. A wooden
# coffee table in the middle carries white cups, plates of biscuits, a teapot, a
# candle and a vase of pink tulips; a patterned rug lies under it and books are
# stacked on its lower shelf. Behind them: a bookcase full of books, framed
# photos on the wall, a turntable and a TV on the right, a dining table and a
# daylight window on the left, plants and lit lamps everywhere.
#
# The photo's coverage is split across three sub-lessons, one per exam task:
#   01 — WOU / WAT: what kind of place it is, what the people are doing, and the
#        objects with their positions.                       ← this file
#   02 — WIEN:      who is there, and one person in visual detail.
#   03 — WÉINI:     time of day and season — hedged, because the scene is indoors.
#
# PURE DESCRIPTION. No @question examiner prompts and no first-person opinion,
# preference or feeling — those belong to the topic themes. A depicted person's
# *visible* emotion is fine: that is observation. Rule of thumb — if a stranger
# could not check the sentence against the photo, it does not belong here.
#
# Held to A1–A2 like the Schueberfouer theme: present tense, simple main
# clauses, spatial adverbs and prepositions, no attributive adjectives on
# masculine or neuter nouns. This is the file that names the setting, which is
# the examiner's first question — `d'Stuff`, `an engem Haus`, not just a list of
# furniture.
#
# The three @fill blocks carry the frames that recur in any interior photo (the
# `Et gi vill …` inventory opener, `ënnert dem …` for position, and the
# what-are-they-doing frame), so the fixed frame teaches the pattern while the
# blank drills the scene word.

@lesson L1.01 "Living Room: General Description"

# The photo all three sub-lessons describe. Pre-cropped to 16:9 and encoded as
# WebP at 880px. @image-alt is the a11y text and doubles as the caption of the
# placeholder frame if the file is ever missing.
@image "/assets/exam/picture/living-room/img/living-room.webp"
@image-alt "A family of four having coffee in a cosy living room: three people on an olive couch, one in an armchair on the right, a wooden coffee table with cups, biscuits, a candle and pink tulips, a bookcase and framed photos behind them, a dining table and a bright window on the left."

# --- The place ---
@word d'Bild = the picture
@word d'Stuff = the living room
@word d'Haus = the house
@word d'Mauer = the wall
@word den Hannergrond = the background

# --- Furniture ---
@word d'Couche = the couch
@word d'Fotell = the armchair
@word de Salonsdësch = the coffee table
@word den Iessdësch = the dining table
@word de Teppech = the carpet
@word d'Bicherregal = the bookcase

# --- Objects ---
@word d'Bicher = the books
@word d'Fotoen = the photos
@word d'Planz = the plant
@word d'Tulpen = the tulips
@word d'Taassen = the cups
@word de Kaffi = the coffee
@word d'Kichelcher = the biscuits

# --- Who is there ---
@word d'Famill = the family
@word d'Leit = the people

# --- Positions ---
@word lénks = on the left
@word riets = on the right
@word ënnert = under

# --- Doing things ---
@word gesinn = to see
@word sëtzen = to sit
@word stoen = to stand
@word drénken = to drink
@word schwätzen = to talk

@sentence
@lu D'Famill ass an der Stuff an engem Haus.
@en The family is in the living room in a house.
@distractor-lu Gaart
@distractor-lu Kichen
@distractor-en garden
@distractor-en kitchen

@sentence
@lu D'Leit drénke Kaffi a schwätzen.
@en The people are drinking coffee and talking.
@distractor-lu iessen
@distractor-lu Taassen
@distractor-en eating
@distractor-en cups

@sentence
@lu Ech gesinn eng Couche lénks an eng Fotell riets.
@en I see a couch on the left and an armchair on the right.
@distractor-lu Iessdësch
@distractor-lu Teppech
@distractor-en table
@distractor-en carpet

@sentence
@lu Et gi vill Fotoen un der Mauer.
@en There are a lot of photos on the wall.
@distractor-lu Teppech
@distractor-lu Tulpen
@distractor-en carpet
@distractor-en tulips

@sentence
@lu Am Hannergrond lénks steet en Iessdësch.
@en In the background on the left there is a dining table.
@distractor-lu riets
@distractor-lu Salonsdësch
@distractor-en right
@distractor-en shelf

@sentence
@lu Op dem Salonsdësch stinn d'Taassen an d'Kichelcher.
@en On the coffee table are the cups and the biscuits.
@distractor-lu Bicher
@distractor-lu Fotoen
@distractor-en books
@distractor-en photos

@sentence
@lu Riets stinn d'Bicher am Bicherregal.
@en On the right the books are in the bookcase.
@distractor-lu lénks
@distractor-lu Kichelcher
@distractor-en left
@distractor-en biscuits

@sentence
@lu Ech gesinn d'Tulpen op dem Salonsdësch.
@en The tulips are on the coffee table.
@distractor-lu Planz
@distractor-lu Iessdësch
@distractor-en plant
@distractor-en shelf

# --- Reusable frames (@fill) ---

# The inventory opener. `Et gi vill … an dësem Zëmmer` transfers to any interior
# photo, which is what makes it a frame rather than a sentence about this room.
# `vill` sits in the frame and forces a plural, so both singular distractors are
# wrong in the blank on grammar alone (R4), and `Zëmmer` carries the dative
# `dësem` the learner reads instead of assembling.
@fill
@lu Et gi vill [Bicher] an dësem Zëmmer.
@en There are a lot of [books] in this room.
@distractor-lu Buch
@distractor-lu Planz
@distractor-lu Taass
@distractor-en book
@distractor-en plant
@distractor-en cup

# The position frame. The blank sits after `dem`, so only a masculine or neuter
# noun fits — feminine `Couche` and `Fotell` would need `der` and are ruled out
# grammatically (R4). `dem` also ends in `-m`, so the Eifeler Regel cannot bite.
@fill
@lu Ech gesinn en Teppech ënnert dem [Dësch].
@en I see a carpet under the [table].
@distractor-lu Couche
@distractor-lu Fotell
@distractor-lu Planz
@distractor-en couch
@distractor-en armchair
@distractor-en plant

# The what-are-they-doing frame. Blank is the verb, and the plural subject
# `d'Leit` is what makes the singular `drénkt` wrong — the sharpest distractor
# here, since the two nouns cannot occupy a verb slot at all (R3).
@fill
@lu Op dem Bild [drénken] d'Leit Kaffi.
@en In the picture the people [are drinking] coffee.
@distractor-lu drénkt
@distractor-lu Taassen
@distractor-lu Kichelcher
@distractor-en drinks
@distractor-en cups
@distractor-en biscuits
